{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Request Object in ASP.NET"}, {"type": "position", "content": 6}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about the Request Object in ASP.NET. "}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Understand information about web applications, their principles and basic concepts in a range of work contexts.  Describe applications for an operating system for personal computers in a range of work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on web applications, and applications for operating systems for personal computers."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob06re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "value "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a variable declared in order to store or point data "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The Request Object is the one in charge of recovering the values or abstractions sent by the client browser to the server."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob06aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "load "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make information appear on a website or application interface "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: These values enable browsers to load pages."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob06aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "instance "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a specification of an object in object-oriented programming "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun):"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: This kind of objects is wrapped by an instance of System.Web.HttpRequest class."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob06aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "retrieve"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to locate and recover something "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We have, for instance, Request.BinaryRead to retrieve and store data sent in a Post request."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob06aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "cookie "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a small piece of data that has information about a user"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Request.Cookies Collection which makes reference to the value of cookies sent in HTTP request."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob06aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "query string "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a set of variables defined in a URL to request pages or applications "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Request.QueryString that is related to all HTTP query string variables."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob06aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Infinitives"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use <strong>verbs followed by to + infinitive</strong> to communicate necessity, obligation, desire or suggestion and sometimes to give an opinion. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "We use <strong>to + infinitive</strong> in these specific cases: </br></br>  1. After certain verbs: </br></br>  These verbs may vary according to their communicative intention: </br></br>  \uf0d8\t<i>Verbs of thinking or feeling</i>: decide, choose, hope, like, prefer, expect, etc. </br> \uf0d8\t<i>Verbs of speaking</i>: promise, agree, refuse, etc. </br>  \uf0d8\t<i>Verbs of purpose</i>: have, need, plan, try, refuse, agree, etc. </br> \uf0d8\t<i>Other verbs</i>: want, help, manage, etc. </br></br>   Examples: </br></br> <i>\u2022\t We have <strong>decided to use </strong>a different app. </br> \u2022\tThe company is <strong>planning to develop</strong> our application soon. </br> \u2022\tThis directive <strong>attempts to make</strong> the application run. </br> \u2022\tWe <strong>agreed to choose </strong>another development platform. </i></br></br>   "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I will try to explain all aspects regarding this object."}, {"type": "example", "content": "It's important to know that when a user sends an HTTP request, the Request Object is the one in charge of recovering the values or abstractions sent by the client browser to the server.  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "It depends on the type of function that we aim to set."}, {"type": "text", "content": "2.  After adjectives: </br></br>  Examples: </br></br>  \u2022\t<i>It's <strong>important to get </strong>this topic. </br> \u2022\tIt's <strong>difficult to code </strong>certain application functions in ASP.NET. </br> \u2022\tSome methods are <strong>complicated to understand</strong>.</i> </br></br>  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "3. To express a purpose: </br></br>  Examples: </br></br>  \u2022\t<i>This object is declared<strong> to load </strong>pages. </br> \u2022\tIts main function is <strong>to retrieve </strong>values from the server. </br> \u2022\tThis is done<strong> to set </strong>different functions. </i>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Simple Present Tense in Third-Person Singular "}, {"type": "describer", "content": "The <strong>simple present tense</strong> is frequently used to talk about routines, permanent situations or general truths. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "These are some examples: </br></br>  Routines: </br></br>  \u2022\t<i>The company frequently <strong>uses</strong> ASP.NET to create applications.</i> </br></br>  Permanent situations: </br></br>  \u2022\t<i>He<strong> works at</strong> a software development company. </i></br></br>  General truths: <i></br></br>  \u2022\tAn application <strong>fails</strong>, every time there is an error in the source code. </i></br></br>  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "It depends on the type of function that we aim to set, so we need to focus on methods and properties that can be defined by the Request Object."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Request.Cookies Collection which makes reference to the value of cookies sent in HTTP request. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "UserHostAddress, that gets the address of a remote client."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When we use the present simple in third-person singular (he, she, it), the main verb always is modified from its base form in the following cases: </br></br>  1)\tIf the verb ends with a <strong>consonant</strong>, you need to add the letter <b>-s</b> to the verb. </br></br>  Example:</br> </br> <i> \u2022\t It <strong>gets</strong> all the information.</i> </br></br>  2)\tIf the verb ends in <strong>-sh, -ch, -ss, -x</strong> or <strong>-o</strong>, you need to add <strong>-es</strong> to the verb. </br> </br> Example: </br> </br> <i>\u2022\tIt <strong>watches</strong> all data and <strong>does</strong> everything to interpret it.</i> </br></br>  3)\tIf the verb ends in <strong>-y</strong>, you need to change it for i and then add \u2013<strong>es</strong>. </br></br> Example: </br> </br> <i>\u2022\tThe server <strong> tries</strong> to collect all variables.</i> </br>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Do some further research about properties and methods of the Request Object in ASP.NET and the way they relate one another. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create a mind map to explain your findings.   "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Use examples to illustrate methods and properties in your explanation.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Prepare an oral presentation to explain your mind map. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Show your findings to the class."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I do further research about properties and methods of the request object in ASP.NET. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I create a mind map to explain my findings.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I use examples to illustrate properties and methods in my explanation. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I prepare an oral presentation to explain my mind map."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I show my findings to my class."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe7n6le01ob06re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "How could we define the Request Object in ASP.NET? Well, during this presentation, I will try to explain all aspects regarding this object. First of all, it's important to know that when a user sends an HTTP request, the Request Object is the one in charge of recovering the values or abstractions sent by the client browser to the server.  These values enable browsers to load pages, which means that this kind of objects is wrapped by an instance of System.Web.HttpRequest class. </br>\nBut how do we set the Request Object when coding? It depends on the type of function that we aim to set, so we need to focus on methods and properties that can be defined by the Request Object.\nMethods are related to the operations that can be performed by classes or objects, and properties, for their part, are fields of objects or classes. </br>"}, {"time": "66000", "image": "enpe7n6le01ob06re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "What methods and properties can the Request Object specify? Concerning methods for the Request Object. We have, for instance, Request.BinaryRead to retrieve and store data sent in a Post request. We can also mention SaveaAS to keep a record of requests in a disk, GetType to define the type of an instance, ValidateInput to provide security, and Map Image Coordinate to describe the image-field parameters, among others. </br>"}, {"time": "102500", "image": "enpe7n6le01ob06re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "In terms of properties, it is possible to bring up different examples: Request.Cookies Collection which makes reference to the value of cookies sent in HTTP request, Request.Form Collection which gathers all form values, Request.QueryString that is related to all HTTP query string variables, and UserHostAddress, that gets the address of a remote client."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n6le01ob06re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n6le01ob06re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe7n6le01ob06re5vsc01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "", "id": 0, "text": ""}]}, "type": "video"}], "type": "video", "_data": [], "id": 2, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can understand written information about properties and methods of the request object in ASP.NET.  "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can use terms related to the request object in ASP.NET.  "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can write questions and answers to check my understanding on the request object in ASP.NET."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Do you think it 